<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

/**
 * The measured language-server matrices: capabilities first, then hover
 * type conformance. Everything here was observed by launching the server —
 * the claims table above this section is where self-description lives, and
 * the two must not blur. Cell vocabulary and classes are decided by
 * SummaryReport; this template only lays them out.
 *
 * @var list<array{name: string, version: string, versionFull: string}> $tools
 * @var list<array{label: string, cells: list<array{class: string, text: string, note: string}>}> $capabilityRows
 * @var list<array{feature: string, expected: string, cells: list<array{class: string, text: string, note: string}>}> $hoverRows
 * @var string $navCorpus                e.g. "psy/psysh@0e8ec1af5990"; '' when no navigation run is committed
 * @var array<string, string> $navFailures tool => session failure reason
 * @var list<array{kind: string, name: string, cells: list<array{class: string, text: string, note: string}>}> $navDefRows
 * @var list<array{kind: string, name: string, cells: list<array{class: string, text: string, note: string}>}> $navRefRows
 */

?>
<h2 class="section" id="language-server-capabilities">Language server capabilities &mdash; measured</h2>
<p class="section-note">Everything in this section comes from launching each server headless against a small fixture workspace: no cell repeats a claim from a README. <strong>Advertised</strong> is what the server&rsquo;s own <code>initialize</code> response declared; the probe verdict says what happened when the suite then called the capability at a position prepared for it. A dash means the handshake did not advertise the capability. The two freemium servers gate paid features in opposite ways, and the matrix shows both faithfully: a free Intelephense <em>omits</em> them from its handshake (they render as dashes), while DEVSENSE <em>advertises</em> them and refuses the call by licence (they render as <strong>Gated</strong>). Hover any cell for what it does and does not claim.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="analyzer-meta">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Capability</th>
<?php foreach ($tools as $tool): ?>
<th scope="col"><?= h($tool['name']) ?><br><small><?= h_noted($tool['version'], $tool['versionFull'] === $tool['version'] ? '' : $tool['versionFull']) ?></small></th>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tr></thead><tbody>
<?php foreach ($capabilityRows as $row): ?>
<tr><th class="tool-name" scope="row"><?= h($row['label']) ?></th>
<?php foreach ($row['cells'] as $cell): ?>
<td class="<?= h($cell['class']) ?> status-cell"><?= h_noted($cell['text'], $cell['note']) ?></td>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tr>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3 class="section" id="hover-conformance">Hover type conformance</h3>
<p class="section-note">The typing question, asked over the protocol: hovering a variable whose type the annotation or the narrowing has already established, does the server show that type? <strong>Precise</strong> means the shown type matches the expected spelling; <strong>Widened / other</strong> means hover answered with something else &mdash; typically the declared type where the narrowed one was expected, or a widened fallback &mdash; and the cell&rsquo;s hover note quotes it verbatim.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="analyzer-meta">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Expected type</th>
<?php foreach ($tools as $tool): ?>
<th scope="col"><?= h($tool['name']) ?></th>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tr></thead><tbody>
<?php foreach ($hoverRows as $row): ?>
<tr><th class="tool-name" scope="row"><?= h($row['feature']) ?></th><td><code><?= h($row['expected']) ?></code></td>
<?php foreach ($row['cells'] as $cell): ?>
<td class="<?= h($cell['class']) ?> status-cell"><?= h_noted($cell['text'], $cell['note']) ?></td>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tr>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tbody></table></div>
<?php if ($navDefRows !== []): ?>

<h3 class="section" id="navigation">Navigating a real project&rsquo;s dependency graph</h3>
<p class="section-note">The corpus is a real project &mdash; <code><?= h($navCorpus) ?></code> from the steins-survey checkout &mdash; and each row is one symbol in it, chosen per kind. <strong>Go to definition</strong> jumps from a use site and must land on the declaration; <strong>find references</strong> asks from the declaration and is scored against the complete, grep-verified set of code references, with only the two files involved in each probe opened &mdash; everything else must come out of the server&rsquo;s own index. A <em>+n</em> after the score counts locations beyond the expected set: the corpus contains same-name decoys (another <code>info()</code>, other <code>getAll()</code>s), so extras expose reference implementations that match by name rather than by symbol. The corpus defines no namespace-level constant that code references &mdash; true of much modern PHP &mdash; so that kind awaits a <code>define()</code>-era corpus.<?php if ($navFailures !== []): ?> Session notes: <?php $parts = []; foreach ($navFailures as $tool => $reason) { $parts[] = h($tool) . ' &mdash; ' . h($reason); } echo implode('; ', $parts); ?>.<?php endif; ?></p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="analyzer-meta">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Go to definition&hellip;</th>
<?php foreach ($tools as $tool): ?>
<th scope="col"><?= h($tool['name']) ?></th>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tr></thead><tbody>
<?php foreach ($navDefRows as $row): ?>
<tr><th class="tool-name" scope="row"><?= h_noted($row['kind'], $row['name']) ?></th>
<?php foreach ($row['cells'] as $cell): ?>
<td class="<?= h($cell['class']) ?> status-cell"><?= h_noted($cell['text'], $cell['note']) ?></td>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tr>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="analyzer-meta">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Find references&hellip;</th>
<?php foreach ($tools as $tool): ?>
<th scope="col"><?= h($tool['name']) ?></th>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tr></thead><tbody>
<?php foreach ($navRefRows as $row): ?>
<tr><th class="tool-name" scope="row"><?= h_noted($row['kind'], $row['name']) ?></th>
<?php foreach ($row['cells'] as $cell): ?>
<td class="<?= h($cell['class']) ?> status-cell"><?= h_noted($cell['text'], $cell['note']) ?></td>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tr>
<?php endforeach; ?>
</tbody></table></div>
<?php endif; ?>
